<h2>Why the moratorium reaches the Facilitation Council</h2>
<p>Section 14 of the IBC is drafted in deliberately wide terms: on admission, the adjudicating authority prohibits &ldquo;the institution of suits or continuation of pending suits or proceedings against the corporate debtor including execution of any judgment, decree or order in any court of law, tribunal, arbitration panel or other authority.&rdquo; A Facilitation Council reference seeks a money recovery from the buyer; the arbitration it ripens into under Section 18(3) is precisely a proceeding before an arbitration panel. The words leave no serious room for an MSME-shaped exception, and Section 238 of the IBC — &ldquo;notwithstanding anything inconsistent therewith contained in any other law&rdquo; — settles the order of precedence between the two special statutes.</p>

<h2>The supplier&rsquo;s working checklist</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Watch for the public announcement.</strong> Admission into CIRP is followed by a public announcement inviting claims. Monitor the NCLT cause lists and the IBBI announcements if the buyer&rsquo;s payment behaviour or market reports suggest insolvency is near.</p>
  <p><strong>File as an operational creditor, in time.</strong> Submit the claim in the prescribed form to the interim resolution professional with invoices, purchase orders, proof of delivery or service, the buyer&rsquo;s acknowledgments, Udyam registration, and the MSMED interest computation. Late claims create avoidable controversy; extinguishment after plan approval is unforgiving.</p>
  <p><strong>Quantify interest correctly.</strong> The MSMED Act&rsquo;s compound interest at three times the bank rate is part of the claim&rsquo;s quantification. State principal and statutory interest separately, with the computation sheet.</p>
  <p><strong>Keep the MSEFC record alive.</strong> Inform the Council of the moratorium and seek adjournment sine die rather than withdrawal, so the reference can revive if the moratorium lifts without a plan — for instance, on liquidation of the CIRP being set aside, or against any party not protected by it.</p>
  <p><strong>Check for guarantees and co-obligors.</strong> The moratorium protects the corporate debtor alone. Director or promoter personal guarantees, and liabilities of entities outside the CIRP, can be pursued on their own terms.</p>
</div>

<h2>After the CIRP: three endings</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Resolution plan approved.</strong> The supplier receives the plan&rsquo;s provision for operational creditors — by law not less than liquidation value. Claims filed but not provided for are extinguished against the revived company; claims never filed fare no better. This is why timely filing is the whole game.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Liquidation.</strong> The claim shifts to the liquidator and takes its place in the Section 53 waterfall, where operational creditors rank after secured creditors and workmen. Realistic expectations and early filing again matter most.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Withdrawal or settlement of the CIRP.</strong> If the insolvency is withdrawn or set aside, the moratorium goes with it, and the paused MSEFC reference or arbitration can be revived from where it stood.</div>
</div>

<h2>Planning before the storm</h2>
<p>For suppliers with concentrated exposure to a single buyer, the best protection is contractual and operational: shorter credit cycles within the statutory forty-five days, personal or bank guarantees where bargaining power allows, invoice discounting through TReDS to move receivables off the books, and prompt invocation of the Samadhaan mechanism at the first sustained default rather than after months of accumulation. The MSMED Act gives a supplier speed; insolvency rewards those who used it early. Once CIRP intervenes, the remedy changes shape — from adjudication to participation — and the supplier&rsquo;s diligence in the claims process becomes the measure of recovery.</p>
